--
Currently: BOTH assertions FAIL -- a wrong verdict on a correct program
(soundness-relevant, unlike the vacuous-drop KNOWNBUGs).  The source is
ten lines; only the libc++-23 headers are needed to reproduce, hence
the preprocessed carrier.
ROUND-90 DIAGNOSIS (bisected):
- An EMPTY vector is modelled correctly (size()==0, empty()).
- `reserve(4)` has NO EFFECT: capacity() is still 0 afterwards
  (asserting capacity()==0 SUCCEEDS), while size() stays correct.
- Root: `__vector_layout<T,A>::__relocate`, which reserve calls, is
  present in the symbol table with an EMPTY body and return type
  `auto (...) -> void` although it is declared `void` -- the OUT-OF-LINE
  definition (layout.h:290) was never matched to the in-class
  declaration (layout.h:196), so the odr-used member has no body
  ([temp.inst]/1 requires implicit instantiation when odr-used).
- The call is NOT flagged and the wrong answer is SILENT.  Round-91
  follow-up explains why twice over: goto-symex emits "no body for
  callee" only when unwinding assertions are enabled
  (symex_function_call.cpp), and this test needs
  --no-unwinding-assertions; but even WITH them enabled no property
  appears, because the callee has NO goto-function entry at all (only
  the CALL instruction exists), so the missing-body path is never
  reached.  A call to a function with no entry silently does nothing --
  a separable soundness gap worth its own fix.
- NEGATIVE KERNELS (round 90, both VERIFY clean): oo1 -- out-of-line
  definition of a class-template member whose parameter type is a
  MEMBER ALIAS; oo2 -- the same with `constexpr` plus libc++'s three
  attributes (visibility / exclude_from_explicit_instantiation /
  abi_tag) on the in-class declaration only.  So neither the member
  alias nor the attribute asymmetry is the trigger.
- FIVE negative kernels now (oo1-oo3 round 90; sk1, sk2 round 91): the
  full skeleton recovered by cvise -- vector holding a
  `__vector_layout` member, `reserve` defined OUT OF LINE calling
  `layout_.relocate(v)`, the parameter type a member alias to a
  forward-declared 3-parameter template whose third argument is a
  template TEMPLATE parameter used CRTP-style, definitions supplied
  after the declarations -- VERIFIES CLEAN when hand-written.  So the
  trigger needs something still unidentified from the real headers.
- ROUND-93 CAUSAL CHAIN, now complete (probes into the deferral,
  odr-use, recovery and overload paths):
    1. the out-of-line definition IS parsed (qualified, with body) and
       the member IS deferred with that body attached;
    2. converting the body FAILS: at layout.h:409 the inner call
       `__buffer.__relocate(__begin_, __end_, __capacity_)` reports
       "found no match for symbol '__relocate'", although the single
       candidate `void __relocate(__split_buffer_pointer_layout *,
       int *&, int *&, int *&)` matches in arity and the arguments are
       the layout's own `int *` members;
    3. the no-viable-call recovery then NILS the body (by design), so an
       odr-used member is left bodyless;
    4. a bodyless call is a NO-OP (KNOWNBUG bodyless_call_no_havoc), so
       reserve() silently does nothing and capacity() stays 0.
  The defect to fix is the failing overload resolution in step 2.
  CORRECTION of an earlier note: `auto (...) -> void` in the symbol dump
  is merely CBMC's printer for code types (a WORKING member prints
  identically), not a deduced return type; and find_out_of_line_body is
  not called because it is only a FALLBACK for bodies that fail to
  convert after being attached by a different route.
- EIGHT negative kernels (oo1-oo3, sk1, sk2, lv1-lv3): all verify clean,
  including passing own members as pointer-REFERENCE arguments to a
  member inherited through a TT-parameter CRTP base with PRIVATE
  inheritance plus friendship.  Hence the machine reduction.
- A reduction with a gate that pins the driver, requires the
  out-of-line DEFINITION text to survive, and requires the member to
  remain bodyless is running in /tmp/cv91.

ROUND-118: cv117's artifact (19 lines) is DEGENERATE -- cvise split
push_back into TWO overloads so the pinned definition text belongs to
the overload NOT selected by `v.push_back(7)` (its const_reference
also lost its const, flipping overload resolution).  GATE LESSON #4:
called-but-undefined checks keyed on a NAME are defeated by overload
sets; a robust pin needs the full signature or a uniquely-named
function.  DO NOT re-reduce blindly: the original carrier has TWO
VISIBLE front-end errors that are the actual lead --
  * conditional.h:40: instantiating 'std::__1::vector' ...
  * __split_buffer:721 function ~_ConstructTransaction: instantiating
    'std::__1::vector' ...
and three vector members (push_back/size/operator[]) end up bodyless.
Next: probe the ~_ConstructTransaction error directly (RESOLVE-THROW /
RTHROW kit) -- visible errors need no reduction.

ROUND-119: FIRST visible error fixed (trailing zero-length-pack
sentinel counted against packless vector<_Tp, _Allocator>; sentinels
now stripped in typecheck_template_args when the target has no trailing
pack).  Probe trail: TMA probe showed vector (2 params) receiving
[cpp_name][struct_tag][ambiguous:empty]; the sentinel is CBMC's own
[temp.variadic]/7 recording convention, and it arrives AMBIGUOUS-
wrapped (first strip attempt matched only ID_type and missed).  The
NEXT layer is now exposed: `found no match for symbol '_Bp'` (twice)
resolving libc++'s `_If` alias -- `typename _IfImpl<_Cond>::template
_Select<_IfRes, _ElseRes>` with bool NTTPs -- plus the residual
__set_sentinel overload mismatch in ~_ConstructTransaction (implicit
object argument `struct __split_buffer` vs candidates on
`__split_buffer_pointer_layout*`: derived-to-base `this` conversion
through the CRTP template-template base; hand kernels t1-t4 of the
bare shapes all PASS, so the failing ingredient is narrower).
